WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.338
"Risks and Benefits of
Nicotinamide Riboside (NR), a NAD+ Booster"

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:13.400
NR and NMN seem to be more promising
NAD+ precursors than NA or NAM,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:18.240
since they don’t cause flushing
nor do they directly inhibit sirtuins.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.760
In mice, NR and NMN both
raised liver NAD+ levels,

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:27.440
but of the two, only NR significantly
raised NAD+ in the muscles.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.720
Also, NR is so far the only NAD+ booster
shown to prolong the lifespan of mice.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.520
There have been at least
10 clinical trials of NR,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.200
most showing it can boost human
blood levels of NAD+ by up to 168%.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.200
Note, though, that most doses
used exceeded 300 mg,

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.160
which is the daily dose
approved as safe

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.800
by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration
and European Food Safety Authority.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.840
At the approved dose, blood NAD+ is
boosted more on the order of 50 to 60%,

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:03.480
but no dose was found to affect NAD+ levels
in human muscle (compared to placebo).

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.600
The greater preponderance
of human bioavailability

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.720
and safety data for
NR compared to NMN

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.160
has led some to proclaim NR
as the preferred NAD+ precursor.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.200
And, by some, I mean employees
of a chemical company

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.640
that produces NR for supplements.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.440
The question after all
of these human NR trials

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.840
is have any of them
shown clinical benefit?

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.400
Sadly, no.
Let’s go through the alphabet.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.120
After accounting for
multiple testing,

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.800
randomized, double-blind,
placebo-controlled trials of NR

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.520
in middle-aged or older adults failed to find
any significant benefit over placebo

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:47.160
for artery stiffness or
artery function, BAT activation,

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.080
blood pressure, blood sugar control,
body weight,

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.040
cardiac energy or ejection fraction,
fat burning,

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:01.800
fatty liver, exercise capacity,
fatigue,

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.400
insulin sensitivity,
metabolic flexibility,

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.120
metabolic health,
metabolic rate,

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.080
mitochondrial function
or biogenesis,

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.600
muscle blood flow, upper
or lower body muscle strength,

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.280
pancreatic function or the
release of metabolic hormones,

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:26.600
the treatment of Parkinson’s disease
symptoms, or physical performance.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.120
NR-hawking companies claim
NR is anti-inflammatory,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.640
but in their own study, only 3 out of 10
markers of inflammation were affected

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:35.680
compared to placebo,

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.280
and a subsequent independent study
using the same dose for twice as long

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.680
found zero of 12 markers affected.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:47.080
Remarkably, the opposite was found for
many of these outcomes in rats and mice.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:50.760
In rodents, NR does raise
NAD+ levels in muscle,

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.720
improving insulin sensitivity
and mitochondrial biogenesis,

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.960
and on down much of the list.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:02.120
Why does NR work in rodents but
appear to almost entirely flop in people?

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.800
Some have suggested
inadequate dosing.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.720
The typical dose used
in mouse studies

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.920
Was about twice that used
in many human studies,

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.960
but a double dose has been
tried in people, to no avail.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:19.760
Another possibility is sirtuin inhibition by NAM,
the main degradation product of NR.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:21.360
In fact, based on mouse studies,

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:27.760
NR may metabolize in the gut into NAM or
NA before it even makes it in the bloodstream.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.240
Either way, unlike in mice,

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.360
NR can’t seem to elevate
NAD+ in human muscle;

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:36.240
so, no wonder there’s no alteration
of human sirtuin activity.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.640
Maybe that explains
the disparate results.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.040
In fact, the key NAD+ synthesizing
enzyme in human muscle biopsies

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:45.640
was actually suppressed
by NR supplementation.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:49.360
This doesn’t happen in mice,
but it does in people.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.480
Presumably this downregulation
is an adaptive response

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.800
to the unnaturally large flood
of NR coming into the system.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.920
So, what happens when you
stop taking the supplement?

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.960
How quickly does your enzyme
activity bounce back?

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:08.240
In mice, not only may
their microbiome affect NR,

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:12.000
but the NR may affect
their microbiome, too.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.880
Some of the benefits of NR
can then be transferred

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.720
between mice via
fecal transplants.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:20.640
So, at least in mice,
some of the benefits of NR

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.120
may be due to modulating
the mouse microbiome.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:27.120
The distinct differences between the
gut flora of humans and rodents

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:32.080
may offer another explanation why
NR works in them, but not us.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.920
Unlike NAM, supplementation with
NR did not increase homocysteine levels,

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:42.200
but one study of a combination of NR
plus a resveratrol analogue called pterostilbene

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:48.520
raised LDL cholesterol high enough to kill
as many as 1 in 40 long-term users.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:51.560
However, this effect is presumed
to be due to the pterostilbene,

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:57.760
as NR alone has not been shown to raise LDL,
whereas pterostilbene has.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:02.040
One study did find that NR seems to
cause a small reduction in hematocrit,

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:06.200
hemoglobin, and platelet count in
people within a week of starting it.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.600
This shift towards a
more anemic state

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:14.280
was suggested to account for impaired
exercise performance seen in rats given NR.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:19.360
However, the 35% drop in performance
did not reach statistical significance.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:23.880
NR did cause a significant increase
in systemic oxidative stress, however,

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.560
and another rodent study
found a worsening of inflammation

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:29.360
and deterioration of
metabolic health,

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:32.880
but if positive effects in rodents
don’t translate to people,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.880
perhaps we shouldn’t expect
that negative ones will either.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.200
Regulatory authorities
from Australia, Canada,

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.640
Europe, and the United States
have all authorized NR as safe,

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:47.840
at least up to 300mg a day
(or 230 in pregnant and lactating women).

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:56.049
But the lack of demonstrable clinical benefit
would seem to preclude NR supplementation.

